On Nov 21, 9:47 pm, James <james.ka...@gmail.com> wrote:

> This may sound very strange coming from a professional Unix
> programmer, and Unix expert, but if your goal is first and
> foremost to learn C, then I'd suggest downloading the free,
> Microsoft Visual Studios 2005 version of C++ (and the SDK
> libraries, since they aren't bundled into the system as they are
> under Unix).  It's a very restrictive environment, and I know of
> no professional (Windows programmers included) who uses it, but
> it does hide a lot of the underlying aspects from you.  Sooner
> or later, of course, you'll have to address them, but there's no
> use trying to learn everything at once.

Oh no, it does not surprise me at all. Someone who looks at open
source projects and thinks, for instance, "OK, looks like they all use
autoconf and stuff. I guess that's the easiest way", will be
frustrated. But there may be free alternatives to MSVC. How about
Eclipse? I haven't used it for C/C++, but for Java and PHP it works
reasonably well for me.

> One thing you might want to do fairly soon is to learn a good
> editor.  The learning process can be painful, and is likely to
> get in the way of learning C some of the times, but the editor
> is the one tool you'll be constantly using, and it's worth
> becoming proficient in a powerful editor early, because of the
> time it will save you once you do learn it.

I disagree with that. The time it takes getting used to non-standard
key bindings and conventions (and mentally switching back and forth)
would take a long time to win back in milliseconds at keystrokes, and
there is some evidence that regular mouse-based editors may even be
better at that, too.

Higher-level features is another matter. They do really save time. So
I suggest an IDE, or maybe a plugin-rich editor. Emacs is the crown
example, but JEdit is designed with CUA key bindings (which will save
you a lot of learning time), easier plugin installation - but merely a
ton of plugins, as opposed to fifty.


